{"title":"KVPY","category":"Engineering","bank":"kvpy","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Redox Reactions","count":16,"questions":[{"id":51312,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Redox Reactions","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"A redox reaction among the following is","options":["$\\mathrm{CdCl}_{2}+2 \\mathrm{KOH} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{Cd}(\\mathrm{OH})_{2}+2 \\mathrm{KCl}$","$\\mathrm{BaCl}_{2}+\\mathrm{K}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{BaSO}_{4}+2 \\mathrm{KCl}$","$\\mathrm{CaCO}_{3} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{CaO}+\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$","$2 \\mathrm{Ca}+\\mathrm{O}_{2} \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm{CaO}$"],"answer":3,"solution":"Redox reaction is the reaction in which oxidation \\& reduction take place simultaneously So answer is (D)\n<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252FGgqFY2-V3oiuCXCtmYA-FF8KkxjMkjHAh3SwEhkupIU.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FGgqFY2-V3oiuCXCtmYA-FF8KkxjMkjHAh3SwEhkupIU.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FGgqFY2-V3oiuCXCtmYA-FF8KkxjMkjHAh3SwEhkupIU.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\">","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2016 (SA)","_marksId":"6174d213fc0d643701f1aae3"},{"id":51313,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Redox Reactions","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"The number of electrons required to reduce chromium completely in $\\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{7}{ }^{2-}$ to $\\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}$ in acidic medium, is","options":["5","3","6","2"],"answer":2,"solution":"$\\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{7}^{2-}+14 \\mathrm{H}^{+}+6 \\mathrm{e}^{-} \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}+7 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2015 (SA)","_marksId":"6174d213e108c762d06e433c"},{"id":51314,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Redox Reactions","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"The formal oxidation numbers of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mtext>C</mtext><mtext>r</mtext></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mtext>C</mtext><mtext>l</mtext></math> in the ions <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>Cr</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>7</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>-</mo></mrow></msubsup></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mtext>C</mtext><mtext>l</mtext><msubsup><mtext>O</mtext><mn>3</mn><mo>-</mo></msubsup></math>, respectively are","options":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo> </mo><mo>+</mo><mn>6</mn><mo> </mo><mi>and</mi><mo> </mo><mo>+</mo><mn>7</mn></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>+</mo><mn>7</mn><mo> </mo><mi>and</mi><mo> </mo><mo>+</mo><mn>5</mn></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>+</mo><mn>6</mn><mo> </mo><mi>and</mi><mo> </mo><mo>+</mo><mn>5</mn></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>+</mo><mn>8</mn><mo> </mo><mi>and</mi><mo> </mo><mo>+</mo><mn>7</mn></math>"],"answer":2,"solution":"In <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mtext>C</mtext><msub><mtext>r</mtext><mn>2</mn></msub><msubsup><mtext>O</mtext><mn>7</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>-</mo></mrow></msubsup></math><br />\nLet the oxidation state of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mtext>C</mtext><mtext>r</mtext></math> be <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mtext>x</mtext></math>\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>∴</mo><mo> </mo><mn>2</mn><mfenced><mi>x</mi></mfenced><mo>+</mo><mn>7</mn><mfenced><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo> </mo><mtext>x</mtext><mo>-</mo><mn>14</mn><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo> </mo><mtext>x</mtext><mo>=</mo><mn>12</mn></math><br />\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mtext>x</mtext><mo>=</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>6</mn></math></p>\n\n<p>In <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mtext>C</mtext><mtext>l</mtext><msubsup><mtext>O</mtext><mn>3</mn><mo>-</mo></msubsup></math></p>\n\n<p>Let the oxidation state of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mtext>C</mtext><mtext>l</mtext></math> be <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mtext>x</mtext></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>∴</mo><mo> </mo><mn>1</mn><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x</mi></mfenced><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mfenced><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mtext>x</mtext><mo>-</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></math><br />\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mtext>x</mtext><mo>=</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>5</mn></math></p>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2018 (SA)","_marksId":"614da3fed8858f6ce583ba38"},{"id":51315,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Redox Reactions","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"<p>In a titration experiment, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>10</mn><mo> </mo><mi>mL</mi></math> of an <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>FeCl</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> solution consumed <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>25</mn><mo> </mo><mi>mL</mi></math> of a standard <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>Cr</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>7</mn></msub></math> solution to reach the equivalent point. The standard <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>Cr</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>7</mn></msub></math> solution is prepared by dissolving <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>225</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></math> of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>Cr</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>7</mn></msub></math> in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>250</mn><mo> </mo><mi>mL</mi></math> water. The concentration of the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>FeCl</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> solution is closest to</p>\n\n<p>[Given : molecular weight of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>Cr</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>294</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi><mo> </mo><msup><mi>mol</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math>]</p>","options":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>25</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>50</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>10</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>04</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></math>"],"answer":0,"solution":"<p>In titration at equivalence point, number of equivalents of both reactants must be same.</p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>∴</mo><mo> </mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></math> (normality) of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>FeCl</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mfenced><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mo>×</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></mrow></mfenced><mo> </mo><mi>of</mi><mo> </mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>Cr</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>7</mn></msub></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi><mo> </mo><mi>of</mi><mo> </mo><msub><mi>FeCl</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></math> (normality) of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>Cr</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo> </mo><mi>Mass</mi><mo> </mo><mo>×</mo><mn>1000</mn></mrow><mrow><mo> </mo><mi>Equivalent</mi><mo> </mo><mi>mass</mi><mo> </mo><mo>×</mo><mo> </mo><mi>Volume</mi><mo> </mo><mi>of</mi><mo> </mo><mi>solution</mi><mo> </mo><mi>prepared</mi></mrow></mfrac></math></p>\n\n<p>Equivalent mass <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo> </mo><mi>Molar</mi><mo> </mo><mi>mass</mi><mo> </mo></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mo> </mo><mi>factor</mi><mo> </mo></mrow></mfrac></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>294</mn><mn>6</mn></mfrac></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>49</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>equi</mi><mo>.</mo></math></p>\n\n<p>(<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>Cr</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>7</mn></msub></math> as oxidising agent is reduced to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>Cr</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math>).</p>\n\n<p>Oxidation state of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn></math> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Cr</mi></math> atoms change from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>+</mo><mn>6</mn></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></math>.</p>\n\n<p>Total change is<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></math>, (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>∴</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi></math>-factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></math>)</p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>∴</mo><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></math> (of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>Cr</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>7</mn></msub></math>) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1225</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1000</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>49</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>250</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>∴</mo><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></math> (of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>FeCl</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>25</mn></mrow><mn>10</mn></mfrac></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>25</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></math></p>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2019 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"614da707837db57ba563f15f"},{"id":51316,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Redox Reactions","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"The oxidation states of $\\mathrm{P}$ atom in $\\mathrm{POCl}_{3}, \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{PO}_{3}$ and $\\mathrm{H}_{4} \\mathrm{P}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{6}$, respectively, are","options":["$+5,+4,+4$","$+5,+5,+4$","$+4,+4,+5$","$+3,+4,+5$"],"answer":0,"solution":"O. No.\n$\\begin{array}{l}\n\\mathrm{POCl}_{3}+5 \\\\\n\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{PO}_{3}+5 \\\\\n\\mathrm{H}_{4} \\mathrm{P}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{6}+4\n\\end{array}$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2017 (19 Nov SA)","_marksId":"6174d2138369e1bb786cbc35"},{"id":51317,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Redox Reactions","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"The Metal with the highest oxidation state present in $\\mathrm{K}_{2} \\mathrm{CrO}_{4}, \\mathrm{NbCl}_{5}$ and $\\mathrm{MnO}_{2}$ is :","options":["$\\mathrm{Nb}$","$\\mathrm{Mn}$","K","$\\mathrm{Cr}$"],"answer":3,"solution":"$+6$ is maximum oxidation state of $\\mathrm{Cr}$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2014 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d213fb60eb47e1d017d8"},{"id":51318,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Redox Reactions","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"In alkaline medium, the reaction of hydrogen peroxide with potassium permanganate produces a compound in which the oxidation state of $\\mathrm{Mn}$ is","options":["$0$","$+2$","$+3$","+4"],"answer":3,"solution":"\n2 \\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-}(\\mathrm{aq})+3 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{2}(\\mathrm{aq}) \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm{MnO}_{2}(\\mathrm{~s})+2 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}(\\mathrm{l})+3 \\mathrm{O}_{2}(\\mathrm{~g})+2 \\mathrm{OH}^{-}{ }_{(\\mathrm{aq})}\n\nOxidation state of $\\mathrm{Mn}$ in $\\mathrm{MnO}_{2}$ is $+4$.","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2015 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d213b9cb4c2f41b98e84"},{"id":51319,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Redox Reactions","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"The amount of $\\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\mathrm{~S}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3} .5 \\mathrm{H}_{2} O$ required to completely reduce $100 \\mathrm{~mL}$ of $0.25 \\mathrm{~N}$ iodine solution, is","options":["$6.20 \\mathrm{~g}$","$9.30 \\mathrm{~g}$","$3.10 \\mathrm{~g}$","$7.75 \\mathrm{~g}$"],"answer":0,"solution":"\n\\mathrm{N}_{1} \\mathrm{~V}_{1}=\\mathrm{N}_{2} \\mathrm{~V}_{2}\n\nmili eq. of hypo $=0.25 \\times 100$\nmili eq. of hypo $=25$\neq. of hypo $=0.025$\nmole of hypo $=0.025 \\times 1\\because \\mathrm{V}_{\\mathrm{f}}=1$\nweight of hypo $=0.025 \\times 248=6.2 \\mathrm{~g}$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2013 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d2139aa660d93f33b911"},{"id":51320,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Redox Reactions","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"The amount of metallic $\\mathrm{Zn}$ (Atomic weight $=65.4)$ required to react with aqueous sodium hydroxide to produce $1 \\mathrm{~g}$ of $\\mathrm{H}_{2}$, is","options":["$32.7 \\mathrm{~g}$","$98.1 \\mathrm{~g}$","$65.4 \\mathrm{~g}$","$16.3 \\mathrm{~g}$"],"answer":0,"solution":"$\\mathrm{Zn}+2 \\mathrm{OH}^{-}+2 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O} \\longrightarrow\\left[\\mathrm{Zn}\\left(\\mathrm{OH}_{4}\\right)\\right]^{2-}+\\mathrm{H}_{2}2 \\mathrm{~gm}(1$ mole $) \\mathrm{H}_{2}$ is given by $65.4 \\mathrm{~gm}$ of $\\mathrm{Zn}1 \\mathrm{gm}$ is given by $\\frac{65.4}{2} \\mathrm{~gm}$ of $\\mathrm{Zn}$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2013 (SA)","_marksId":"6174d213e693ce1d8e806d47"},{"id":51321,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Redox Reactions","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"The number of moles of $\\mathrm{Br}_{2}$ produced when two moles of potassium permanganate are treated with excess potassium bromide in aqueous acid medium is -","options":["5","3","2","4"],"answer":0,"solution":"$\\mathrm{KMnO}_{4}+\\mathrm{KBr}+\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{MnSO}_{4}+\\mathrm{K}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}+\\mathrm{Br}_{2}+\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$\nBalanced equations\n$2 \\mathrm{KMnO}_{4}+10 \\mathrm{KBr}+8 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4} \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm{MnSO}_{4}+5 \\mathrm{Br}_{2}+6 \\mathrm{~K}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}+8 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$\nEq.of $\\mathrm{KMnO}_{4}=\\mathrm{Eq} .$ of $\\mathrm{Br}_{2}$\nMole $\\times \\mathrm{n}=$ Mole $\\times \\mathrm{n}2 \\times 5=\\mathrm{Mole} \\times 2$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2012 (SA)","_marksId":"6174d213ae9f97e90a2b9195"},{"id":51322,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Redox Reactions","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"Upon fully dissolving $2.0 \\mathrm{~g}$ of a metal in sulfuric acid, $6.8 \\mathrm{~g}$ of the metal sulfale is formed. The equivalent weight of the metal is $-$","options":["$13.6 \\mathrm{~g}$","$20.0 \\mathrm{~g}$","$4.0 \\mathrm{~g}$","$10.0 \\mathrm{~g}$"],"answer":1,"solution":"Equivalents of metal = Equivalents of metal sulphate\n$\\frac{\\text { wt.of metal }}{\\text { Eq.wt.of metal }} \\frac{\\text { wt.of metal sulphate }}{\\text { Eq. wt.metal sulphate }}\\begin{array}{l}\n\\frac{2}{x}=\\frac{6.8}{x+48} \\\\\n6.8 x=2 x+96 \\\\\n4.8 x=96 \\\\\nx=\\frac{96}{4.8}=20\n\\end{array}$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2012 (SA)","_marksId":"6174d21366a2a56751f02576"},{"id":51323,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Redox Reactions","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"Which of the following is NOT an oxidationreduction reaction?","options":["$\\mathrm{H}_{2}+\\mathrm{Br}_{2} \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm{HBr}$","$\\mathrm{NaCl}+\\mathrm{AgNO}_{3} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{NaNO}_{3}+\\mathrm{AgCl}$","$2 \\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\mathrm{~S}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3}+\\mathrm{I}_{2} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\mathrm{~S}_{4} \\mathrm{O}_{6}+2 \\mathrm{NaI}$","$\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}+\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{HCl}+\\mathrm{HOCl}$"],"answer":1,"solution":"$\\mathrm{NaCl}+\\mathrm{AgNO}_{3} \\longrightarrow \\mathrm{NaNO}_{3}+\\mathrm{AgCl}$ is not a oxidation-reduction reaction because there is no change in oxidation state of any element.","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2017 (5 Nov SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d2139e13d17fb5afce57"},{"id":51324,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Redox Reactions","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"The number of moles of $\\mathrm{KMnO}_{4}$ required to oxidize one equivalent of $\\mathrm{Kl}$ in the presence of sulfuric acid is","options":["5","2","$1 / 2$","$1 / 5$"],"answer":3,"solution":"$\\quad$ Valency factor of $\\mathrm{KMnO}_{4}=55 \\mathrm{e}^{-}+\\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-}+8 \\mathrm{H}^{+} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{Mn}^{+2}+4 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2014 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d213d42b559d44aa866d"},{"id":51325,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Redox Reactions","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"The reaction, $\\mathrm{K}_{2} \\mathrm{cr}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{7}+\\mathrm{m} \\mathrm{FeSO}_{4}+\\mathrm{n} \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\\left(\\mathrm{SO}_{4}\\right)_{3}+\\mathrm{p} \\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\\left(\\mathrm{SO}_{4}\\right)_{3}+\\mathrm{K}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}+\\mathrm{q} \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$ when balanced, $m, n, p$, and $q$ are, respectively","options":["$6,14,3,14$","$6,7,3,7$","$3,7,2,7$","$4,14,2,14$"],"answer":1,"solution":"$\\mathrm{K}_{2} \\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{7}+6 \\mathrm{FeSO}_{4}+7 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4 \\rightarrow} \\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\\left(\\mathrm{SO}_{4}\\right)_{3}+3 \\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\\left(\\mathrm{SO}_{4}\\right)_{3}+\\mathrm{K}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}+7 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2014 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d213eb3ae92d54d8c110"},{"id":51326,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Redox Reactions","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"$\\mathrm{MnO}_{2}$ when fused with $\\mathrm{KOH}$ and oxidized in air gives a dark green compound $\\mathrm{X}$. In acidic $\\mathrm{X}$. In acidic solution, $\\mathrm{X}$ undergoes disproportion to give an intense purple compound $\\mathrm{Y}$ and $\\mathrm{MnO}_{2}$. The compounds $\\mathrm{X}$ and $\\mathrm{Y}$. respectively, are","options":["$\\mathrm{K}_{2} \\mathrm{MnO}_{4}$ and $\\mathrm{KMnO}_{4}$","$\\mathrm{Mn}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{7}$ and $\\mathrm{KMnO}_{4}$","$\\mathrm{K}_{2} \\mathrm{MnO}_{4}$ and $\\mathrm{Mn}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{7}$","$\\mathrm{KMnO}_{4}$ and $\\mathrm{K}_{2} \\mathrm{MnO}_{4}$"],"answer":0,"solution":"$2 \\mathrm{MnO}_{2}+4 \\mathrm{KOH}+\\mathrm{O}_{2} \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm{~K}_{2} \\mathrm{MnO}_{4}+2 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}3 \\mathrm{~K}_{2} \\mathrm{MnO}_{4}+4 \\mathrm{HCl} \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm{KMnO}_{4}+\\mathrm{MnO}_{2}+4 \\mathrm{KCl}+2 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2015 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d213016d9e0fa74fb015"},{"id":51327,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Redox Reactions","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"The oxidation number of sulphur is $+4$ in","options":["$\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{~S}$","$\\mathrm{CS}_{2}$","$\\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}$","$\\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{3}$"],"answer":3,"solution":"$\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{~S}^{-2}\\mathrm{CS}_{2}^{-2}$\n<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252F8XTuK-8USNCfEYU4P4tQJjk-XhS6WOa7-LbNvJry3y4.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2F8XTuK-8USNCfEYU4P4tQJjk-XhS6WOa7-LbNvJry3y4.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2F8XTuK-8USNCf